WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.706
- You know, I don't take for granted

00:00:01.706 --> 00:00:03.259
that people just admire what we do,

00:00:03.259 --> 00:00:04.907
so, whatever we do, they'll admire.

00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:06.234
When people talk about
boredom, for example,

00:00:06.234 --> 00:00:08.007
I can honestly say I've never been bored

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:09.294
for a second in my life.

00:00:09.294 --> 00:00:10.731
But I've always found
another way not to be.

00:00:10.731 --> 00:00:12.892
If you name any band
from kind of the mid-70s,

00:00:12.892 --> 00:00:14.389
we probably opened for them.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:16.060
You can't get together
with three people and say,

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.864
well, we want to have a career spanning

00:00:17.864 --> 00:00:18.822
over 20 years and have a

00:00:18.822 --> 00:00:19.784
really loyal audience.
- Right, and here's the plan.

00:00:19.784 --> 00:00:20.790
- Yeah, here's the plan.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.529
- When people come up and
say something like that,

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:24.686
you influenced me to play drums.

00:00:24.686 --> 00:00:26.623
I say, apologize to your parents for me.

00:00:26.623 --> 00:00:28.343
I stopped reading reviews long ago because

00:00:28.343 --> 00:00:29.690
if I believed the good ones,

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:30.965
I'd have to believe the bad ones.

00:00:30.965 --> 00:00:32.550
It's a good point and most of my

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.710
rhythmic growth is evolutionary.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.533
- He's a Canadian American
musician and author.

00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:40.880
He's best known as a
drummer and primary lyricist

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.674
for the rock band Rush.

00:00:42.674 --> 00:00:45.328
He's received numerous awards
for his musical performances.

00:00:45.328 --> 00:00:48.384
He's Neil Peart and here's my take on his

00:00:48.384 --> 00:00:49.607
top ten rules for success.

00:00:49.607 --> 00:00:51.492
Number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:51.492 --> 00:00:55.659
so I'm curious to find out which
one you guys like the most.

00:00:57.581 --> 00:01:00.187
- You know, I don't take for
granted that people just admire

00:01:00.187 --> 00:01:02.028
what we do, so whatever
we do, they'll admire.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.509
No, you know, I feel like,
in fact, this is very true;

00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:06.889
every audience, I feel
like we have to earn them.

00:01:06.889 --> 00:01:09.400
You know, we have to earn their dedication

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.962
and their expenditure of time and energy

00:01:11.962 --> 00:01:13.414
and money to be there.

00:01:13.414 --> 00:01:14.739
Every single time.

00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:15.894
You know, every show.

00:01:15.894 --> 00:01:17.653
It's an old show business
thing, but I really feel

00:01:17.653 --> 00:01:19.682
that you're only as
good as your last show.

00:01:19.682 --> 00:01:21.403
That's the one thing I
do like about touring.

00:01:21.403 --> 00:01:23.195
If you have a bad one,
you've got another chance.

00:01:23.195 --> 00:01:25.595
- It seems like you're a man of extremes.

00:01:25.595 --> 00:01:26.658
Like, you don't just drum,

00:01:26.658 --> 00:01:29.441
you drum on one of the
largest kits there is.

00:01:29.441 --> 00:01:32.556
You don't just ride motorcycles,
you ride 50,000 miles.

00:01:32.556 --> 00:01:33.993
- I'm an extremist.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:36.771
- Even when you describe your
appetite for reading, too.

00:01:36.771 --> 00:01:39.057
It seems like a voracious
appetite for reading.

00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:43.189
Is that what got you where
you are as a drummer?

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:45.719
- I would expect that it all comes down

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:47.320
to a kind of restlessness.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.296
When people talk about
boredom, for example,

00:01:49.296 --> 00:01:50.858
I can honestly say I've never been bored

00:01:50.858 --> 00:01:52.332
for a second in my life,

00:01:52.332 --> 00:01:54.245
but I've always found
another way not to be.

00:01:54.245 --> 00:01:56.375
If I have to go to a medical appointment

00:01:56.375 --> 00:01:58.109
or something like that,
I bring a book with me.

00:01:58.109 --> 00:01:59.557
So, that waiting is not
going to be a hardship.

00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:00.729
With the early days of touring,

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:02.959
when all that dead time
was hanging on me, reading.

00:02:02.959 --> 00:02:05.670
Portable, useful,
nurturing; all that stuff.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.450
So, I think I combat...

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.950
You learn to adapt to your own personality

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.145
and the extremes do come in there, too.

00:02:14.145 --> 00:02:15.878
I wanted to read every book ever written.

00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:20.665
Drumming-wise, my determination
was more important

00:02:20.665 --> 00:02:22.239
than talent, early on.

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:23.696
I think, for most things,

00:02:23.696 --> 00:02:25.196
you do have to have a bit of a knack,

00:02:25.196 --> 00:02:27.364
but I had to work so hard
to learn so many things,

00:02:27.364 --> 00:02:31.397
but I was relentless in
terms of determination.

00:02:31.397 --> 00:02:33.501
And with traveling, when I've got a day,

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:36.627
I'm tempted by what's the
most excellent thing I can do

00:02:36.627 --> 00:02:39.216
which might be way too
far or way too long,

00:02:39.216 --> 00:02:41.199
but I'm going to go for that because

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:42.858
it's the most excellent thing I can do.

00:02:42.858 --> 00:02:45.705
And with writing, it just seems
unstinting in the same way

00:02:45.705 --> 00:02:50.241
because I just kind of know
when I've done my best on it.

00:02:50.241 --> 00:02:52.336
Maybe an editor can help make it better

00:02:52.336 --> 00:02:54.437
and maybe somebody else
could have written it better,

00:02:54.437 --> 00:02:56.155
but I have a good built-in sense of

00:02:56.155 --> 00:02:57.715
this is the best I can do.

00:02:57.715 --> 00:03:00.213
I kind of accepted the
definition and applied it myself.

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:01.833
Yes, I'm a compositional drummer.

00:03:01.833 --> 00:03:03.500
And then, in the same breath I went,

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.007
wait a minute, I want to be
an improvisational drummer.

00:03:06.007 --> 00:03:06.840
You know?

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.819
And also push myself
out of my comfort zone.

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:13.912
Even my solos, in the past,
were always improvised

00:03:13.912 --> 00:03:15.918
over a carefully structured framework.

00:03:15.918 --> 00:03:17.592
But, I wanted even that to be freer.

00:03:17.592 --> 00:03:20.395
To say, okay, it's a
performance in front of people,

00:03:20.395 --> 00:03:21.998
so it's not going to be like,

00:03:21.998 --> 00:03:23.567
just coming here and playing for

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:24.982
a couple of hours for myself.

00:03:24.982 --> 00:03:26.363
It is a performance and I want it to have

00:03:26.363 --> 00:03:29.140
a certain standard of repeatability.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.144
You know?

00:03:30.144 --> 00:03:32.892
So that it's going to be

00:03:32.892 --> 00:03:33.850
satisfying for me

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:35.712
and for the audience every night.

00:03:35.712 --> 00:03:37.468
So, that's a hard line to walk, too.

00:03:37.468 --> 00:03:41.879
But, I did work over the course
of the tour to achieve that.

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:43.611
And, I think we did

00:03:43.611 --> 00:03:45.837
44 shows on this first part of the

00:03:45.837 --> 00:03:47.835
tour in this summer and this fall.

00:03:47.835 --> 00:03:50.533
Towards the end, I was
getting so outside of...

00:03:50.533 --> 00:03:52.444
I remember I thought, bolero!

00:03:52.444 --> 00:03:55.352
I'm going to put bolero
over top of the shahato.

00:03:55.352 --> 00:03:56.185
And did it!

00:03:56.185 --> 00:03:58.172
What I love about my warm up, too,

00:03:58.172 --> 00:04:00.172
it has the same freedom.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.399
About a half hour before show time,

00:04:03.399 --> 00:04:05.530
I go in and play for about
twenty minutes and just play.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:06.478
It's the same thing.

00:04:06.478 --> 00:04:08.568
I usually use those
three rhythmic elements

00:04:08.568 --> 00:04:10.464
that are my foundation right now.

00:04:10.464 --> 00:04:12.611
But there's no consequences.

00:04:12.611 --> 00:04:14.566
It doesn't matter what happens.

00:04:14.566 --> 00:04:16.446
So I would just, in my warm-up that night,

00:04:16.446 --> 00:04:18.773
I tried putting a bolero over
the top of it and it worked.

00:04:18.773 --> 00:04:20.542
I said, okay, and it went
in my solo that night.

00:04:20.542 --> 00:04:23.335
And that certain freshness that I loved,

00:04:23.335 --> 00:04:25.580
that I'd been building on
more the last couple of tours,

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.089
is being able to do that.

00:04:27.089 --> 00:04:29.545
To say, okay, I've almost got this,

00:04:29.545 --> 00:04:30.580
I've almost got this,

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:31.880
I'm going to try it.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.380
We were an opening act for so long and

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.185
we had a really good relationship with

00:04:35.185 --> 00:04:37.521
the guys in Kiss and their crew.

00:04:37.521 --> 00:04:39.982
We opened for Aerosmith like 40 times

00:04:39.982 --> 00:04:41.840
and never got a sound check.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.870
I mean, Blue Oyster Cult, Ted Nuggent,

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.032
if you name any band
from kind of the mid-70s,

00:04:47.032 --> 00:04:48.382
we probably opened for them,

00:04:48.382 --> 00:04:50.231
because we were an opening act.

00:04:50.231 --> 00:04:51.986
And then we started headlining small halls

00:04:51.986 --> 00:04:54.386
and still be opening shows in between.

00:04:54.386 --> 00:04:56.100
So, we had that mixed bag for a long time,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.078
which really contributed
to our balance, I think.

00:04:59.078 --> 00:05:02.568
We learned a lot about character studies

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.162
from watching other people.

00:05:04.162 --> 00:05:06.446
And some of the lyrics that
I've written on the subject

00:05:06.446 --> 00:05:08.256
and thoughts that I've
expressed on the subject

00:05:08.256 --> 00:05:09.552
of fame and celebrity

00:05:09.552 --> 00:05:11.635
are from witnessing how people like that

00:05:11.635 --> 00:05:13.108
behaved and handled it.

00:05:13.108 --> 00:05:15.021
And, in turn, how they came out.

00:05:15.021 --> 00:05:18.005
- Neil Peart is the drummer,
of course, with Rush.

00:05:18.005 --> 00:05:20.206
30 million albums sold.

00:05:20.206 --> 00:05:21.678
It is quite incredible.

00:05:21.678 --> 00:05:23.139
Over 22 years.

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:26.347
This is a fixture on
the Canadian landscape.

00:05:26.347 --> 00:05:28.236
Do you have any understanding why?

00:05:28.236 --> 00:05:29.539
(laughs)

00:05:29.539 --> 00:05:30.372
- Ah no, it's an often asked--

00:05:30.372 --> 00:05:31.735
- [Interviewer] I mean, of
course you're talented and good.

00:05:31.735 --> 00:05:33.745
- Well, no, obviously we
know that's not enough.

00:05:33.745 --> 00:05:35.242
Even were it true.

00:05:35.242 --> 00:05:36.769
It's an often asked question

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:38.713
and one which no one
knows the real answer.

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:40.367
If they did, they would
bottle it and sell it

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:43.322
to all aspiring young musicians.

00:05:43.322 --> 00:05:44.689
Obviously we're glad that it sold,

00:05:44.689 --> 00:05:46.499
but you can't get together
with three people and say

00:05:46.499 --> 00:05:48.798
we want to have a career
spanning over 20 years and

00:05:48.798 --> 00:05:49.698
have a really loyal audience.

00:05:49.698 --> 00:05:51.810
- Right, and here's the plan.
- Yeah, here's the plan.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.368
So we just did what we thought was right

00:05:53.368 --> 00:05:54.421
and made music that we liked

00:05:54.421 --> 00:05:55.703
and hoped other people would too,

00:05:55.703 --> 00:05:57.378
and so far that's been the secret formula.

00:05:57.378 --> 00:06:00.860
Inspiration, I think,
is the rewarding thing.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.550
When people come up and
say something like that,

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.345
you influenced me to play drums.

00:06:05.345 --> 00:06:07.746
I say, apologize to your parents for me.

00:06:07.746 --> 00:06:08.831
(laughs)

00:06:08.831 --> 00:06:11.169
And music is certainly about
sharing your enthusiasm.

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:13.308
The nature of Rush is
that we love our music

00:06:13.308 --> 00:06:14.912
and we hope other people will like it too.

00:06:14.912 --> 00:06:17.267
There's nothing calculated
more than that about it.

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:19.799
In the writing, too, these
are things that I was moved

00:06:19.799 --> 00:06:23.178
to write about and to
craft carefully enough

00:06:23.178 --> 00:06:25.497
that I hope I can share with the reader.

00:06:25.497 --> 00:06:28.107
If you do infect them with your fire.

00:06:28.107 --> 00:06:29.076
Oh, that's good.

00:06:29.076 --> 00:06:31.027
Enthusiasm, the literal
meaning of enthusiasm is

00:06:31.027 --> 00:06:33.527
infected with the god.

00:06:33.527 --> 00:06:35.256
If you can share that god of yours,

00:06:35.256 --> 00:06:36.490
that fire of yours,

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:37.777
and to light someone else's,

00:06:37.777 --> 00:06:39.123
that's a great thing, too.

00:06:39.123 --> 00:06:40.276
- We talked about the magazine.

00:06:40.276 --> 00:06:42.183
I said you were one of the
best living dramatists.

00:06:42.183 --> 00:06:44.442
Another poet that brings
your lyrics into question,

00:06:44.442 --> 00:06:46.232
how did you feel when you read that?

00:06:46.232 --> 00:06:47.147
- Wow, stunned.

00:06:47.147 --> 00:06:48.127
- They said you weren't
a really good lyricist,

00:06:48.127 --> 00:06:49.941
but I'm sure that people
obviously disagree with it,

00:06:49.941 --> 00:06:51.275
but when you read that

00:06:51.275 --> 00:06:52.225
how do you feel?

00:06:52.225 --> 00:06:54.422
- You know, you can't help but be stunned.

00:06:54.422 --> 00:06:57.345
I don't think that anyone
that I would want to know

00:06:57.345 --> 00:06:59.455
would be above that.

00:06:59.455 --> 00:07:01.554
And it's actually a
beautiful illustration.

00:07:01.554 --> 00:07:03.943
Another author, Tom Robbins, I was always

00:07:03.943 --> 00:07:05.694
a great admirer of his novels

00:07:05.694 --> 00:07:07.300
and I loved the way
they kept getting better

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.847
and more adventurous every time.

00:07:08.847 --> 00:07:11.466
There was one of his, I think
it was "Skinny Legs and All",

00:07:11.466 --> 00:07:13.127
that there was just a scathing review

00:07:13.127 --> 00:07:14.722
in the New York Times by a moron.

00:07:14.722 --> 00:07:18.597
So, I just got angry about
it and I wrote to Tom Robbins

00:07:18.597 --> 00:07:20.681
and said look, as long time
reader I want you to know

00:07:20.681 --> 00:07:22.470
your readers are more than satisfied

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.631
and I love the way you keep
progressing, et cetera.

00:07:24.631 --> 00:07:26.555
He wrote me back a really
nice answer and said

00:07:26.555 --> 00:07:28.356
I stopped reading reviews long ago

00:07:28.356 --> 00:07:29.639
because if I believed the good ones,

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:30.903
I'd have to believe the bad ones.

00:07:30.903 --> 00:07:32.207
I took that sort of to heart

00:07:32.207 --> 00:07:33.633
and I never read another review.

00:07:33.633 --> 00:07:35.656
Well, really, I tend to
write for a general reader

00:07:35.656 --> 00:07:37.582
and in the case of this book, certainly,

00:07:37.582 --> 00:07:39.127
I was writing for someone
who doesn't know anything

00:07:39.127 --> 00:07:43.283
about Rush, or rock, or
motorcycling, or any of that.

00:07:43.283 --> 00:07:45.560
I figure it's my job
to make it interesting,

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.421
as the other travel writers that I admire.

00:07:48.421 --> 00:07:50.186
And in every case, it was easier

00:07:50.186 --> 00:07:51.810
when I was writing about
bicycling in Africa

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:53.376
then it's it's obviously not a Rush book.

00:07:53.376 --> 00:07:55.287
When I'm motorcycling
around the US or driving

00:07:55.287 --> 00:07:56.730
and listening to music.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:58.136
It was about music, but it really wasn't

00:07:58.136 --> 00:08:00.386
about me as a rock drummer.

00:08:01.262 --> 00:08:03.589
This time I used all
of the same approaches

00:08:03.589 --> 00:08:05.136
of my ideal audience.

00:08:05.136 --> 00:08:07.591
Someone who is curious and
intelligent about stuff,

00:08:07.591 --> 00:08:09.600
but again, doesn't
necessarily know anything

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.100
about my preoccupations.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.701
So, I was very clearly writing
for that imaginary audience

00:08:14.701 --> 00:08:16.610
in the same way that songwriting

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:19.301
is for an imaginary listener.

00:08:19.301 --> 00:08:21.091
Or even when I'm rehearsing for a tour,

00:08:21.091 --> 00:08:24.187
it's for an imaginary audience
that I'm gearing myself up.

00:08:24.187 --> 00:08:27.020
It's an ideal sense, but it's also

00:08:28.088 --> 00:08:29.426
based on what I would expect.

00:08:29.426 --> 00:08:31.801
Because I love reading travel books myself

00:08:31.801 --> 00:08:33.505
and about other people's experiences.

00:08:33.505 --> 00:08:34.397
It's a good point.

00:08:34.397 --> 00:08:37.740
Most of my rhythmic
growth is evolutionary.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.568
So, that what I've worked on
grows into something else,

00:08:40.568 --> 00:08:42.082
grows into something else.

00:08:42.082 --> 00:08:43.567
There have been the odd times that

00:08:43.567 --> 00:08:45.264
I would call epiphanies like that.

00:08:45.264 --> 00:08:48.782
There's a rhythm that I used
a lot in the late 80s or so,

00:08:48.782 --> 00:08:52.293
Nigerian music, west African music

00:08:52.293 --> 00:08:54.522
with slightly displaced accents in it.

00:08:54.522 --> 00:08:58.800
I heard that, I was in a
village in Togo, in west Africa.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.197
- On your ten-speed?

00:09:00.197 --> 00:09:02.112
- Yeah, I was traveling
by bicycle that time.

00:09:02.112 --> 00:09:04.112
I just heard this rhythm

00:09:04.995 --> 00:09:07.237
echoing across the valley

00:09:07.237 --> 00:09:11.278
and it was just two drummers
with hand drums playing it.

00:09:11.278 --> 00:09:13.514
It engraved itself on my mind.

00:09:13.514 --> 00:09:15.433
It became a huge influence
on me, rhythmically,

00:09:15.433 --> 00:09:18.521
after that because I had been listening to

00:09:18.521 --> 00:09:20.111
King Sunny Ade, he was a pioneer

00:09:20.111 --> 00:09:22.118
of Nigerian pop music

00:09:22.118 --> 00:09:23.368
and this certain

00:09:23.368 --> 00:09:25.701
rhythmic accent syncopations

00:09:27.071 --> 00:09:28.731
that it was based on that I was

00:09:28.731 --> 00:09:30.901
very influenced by at the time.

00:09:30.901 --> 00:09:33.619
Then, this other rhythm that
I heard that time in Togo

00:09:33.619 --> 00:09:35.702
was from that same heart.

00:09:37.345 --> 00:09:40.542
The music that I heard, it
lodges itself in my brain.

00:09:40.542 --> 00:09:42.875
It engraves itself in there.

00:09:43.821 --> 00:09:45.749
And that becomes something special.

00:09:45.749 --> 00:09:46.948
Those are rare, I think.

00:09:46.948 --> 00:09:49.354
When you're suddenly hit by
something that's that strong.

00:09:49.354 --> 00:09:51.658
Reggae, the first time reggae music really

00:09:51.658 --> 00:09:55.825
got into my soul and I felt
that upbeat, downbeat tension.

00:09:56.888 --> 00:09:58.053
Again, I'll never forget.

00:09:58.053 --> 00:10:00.381
It was a Bob Marley
song, "Natural Mystic".

00:10:00.381 --> 00:10:01.214
- Yeah.

00:10:01.214 --> 00:10:03.530
- Just sitting in a car
and hearing that song

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.867
and suddenly I got it.

00:10:05.867 --> 00:10:07.546
I got what reggae was all about.

00:10:07.546 --> 00:10:09.351
I'd heard it before, but hadn't got it.

00:10:09.351 --> 00:10:11.768
- You write in your book, because you

00:10:11.768 --> 00:10:13.713
were talking about the art of writing,

00:10:13.713 --> 00:10:15.573
you say and planting roots and everything,

00:10:15.573 --> 00:10:17.743
you say magic exists but
often requires some planning.

00:10:17.743 --> 00:10:19.759
But, you were talking about writing.

00:10:19.759 --> 00:10:22.408
I think it also seems
to apply to the music.

00:10:22.408 --> 00:10:24.174
You have some planning, but then there's

00:10:24.174 --> 00:10:25.457
that room for improv.

00:10:25.457 --> 00:10:27.020
- Well said. Absolutely so.

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.625
All that effort and study and I would say

00:10:29.625 --> 00:10:32.157
that the early records that
three of us made together,

00:10:32.157 --> 00:10:33.510
we were experimenting.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:34.977
We were learning how to
play our instruments.

00:10:34.977 --> 00:10:36.501
We were learning how to write songs.

00:10:36.501 --> 00:10:38.233
We were learning how to
arrange, especially, through,

00:10:38.233 --> 00:10:40.281
say, Farewells to Kings Hemispheres.

00:10:40.281 --> 00:10:41.848
We were learning so much
about arrangement and

00:10:41.848 --> 00:10:44.870
orchestration in the sense
of a three-piece rock band.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:47.733
All of that, gathered together,

00:10:47.733 --> 00:10:49.150
and then by, say,

00:10:50.736 --> 00:10:52.469
Moving Pictures, 1980,

00:10:52.469 --> 00:10:54.240
so, we'd been together
six years by that time,

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.907
and then we jelled and
all of those studies,

00:10:57.907 --> 00:11:00.978
all those courses of study
that we went through together

00:11:00.978 --> 00:11:03.642
with pure innocence and pure enthusiasm

00:11:03.642 --> 00:11:06.157
suddenly they became a
unified approach that

00:11:06.157 --> 00:11:09.716
Moving Pictures, and from
then on, carried for us.

00:11:09.716 --> 00:11:12.398
As that time, yeah, even from that time,

00:11:12.398 --> 00:11:14.202
we continued to experiment, absolutely,

00:11:14.202 --> 00:11:16.199
with different songs, different
kinds of arrangements,

00:11:16.199 --> 00:11:19.070
orchestrations, all kinds,
different producers.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:20.690
There's another good example.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:22.958
You can get comfortable
working with the same producer,

00:11:22.958 --> 00:11:24.567
and some people do their whole career,

00:11:24.567 --> 00:11:27.621
but we always thought we
wanted new brains to pick

00:11:27.621 --> 00:11:31.086
and new criticisms, new enthusiasms,

00:11:31.086 --> 00:11:31.919
just new,

00:11:32.961 --> 00:11:34.044
new opinions.

00:11:34.878 --> 00:11:36.067
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:36.067 --> 00:11:38.911
I made this video because
Sam Mack asked me to,

00:11:38.911 --> 00:11:40.542
so, if there's a famous entrepreneur that

00:11:40.542 --> 00:11:42.055
you want me to profile next,

00:11:42.055 --> 00:11:43.392
leave it in the comments below

00:11:43.392 --> 00:11:44.638
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:44.638 --> 00:11:45.863
I'd also love to know which

00:11:45.863 --> 00:11:47.795
of the top ten rules hit you the hardest,

00:11:47.795 --> 00:11:49.920
had the biggest impact on you, and why?

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:51.292
Which one was your favorite?

00:11:51.292 --> 00:11:53.749
Leave it in the comments and
I will join in the discussion.

00:11:53.749 --> 00:11:55.421
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:55.421 --> 00:11:58.921
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

